---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import GifAudioToMp3 from '@/components/tools/media/GifAudioToMp3.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="ogg-to-mp3">
  <GifAudioToMp3
    client:load
    accept=".ogg,.oga,.opus,.ogv,audio/ogg"
    sourceLabel="OGG"
    sourceHint="Vorbis or Opus inside an Ogg container: game audio, Wikipedia clips, voice notes, Linux recordings"
    target="mp3"
    defaultBitrate="192"
    formatNote="Ogg files hold either Vorbis or Opus, and the extension does not say which. Both are decoded here, so you do not need to check first."
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Ogg is a container, not a codec</h2>
    <p>
      People often confuse this. <code>.ogg</code> describes the wrapper. The audio inside is usually
      Vorbis, but since about 2015 it is more and more often Opus. <code>.oga</code> is the formally
      correct extension for Ogg audio, and <code>.ogv</code> is Ogg video. The filename does not tell
      you what the decoder will find.
    </p>
    <p>
      So "my Ogg file won't open" has many different causes. A player that supports Vorbis may not
      support Opus in the same container. This OGG to MP3 converter decodes both, and the few Ogg FLAC
      files in use, so you do not need to know which you have.
    </p>

    <h2>Where Ogg files come from</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Games: Vorbis was the default soundtrack format for a generation of PC games, because it is patent-free and had no licensing fee, unlike MP3 at the time.</li>
      <li>Wikipedia and Wikimedia Commons: their audio is Ogg on principle, because the projects require freely licensed, patent-free formats.</li>
      <li>Spotify: streams are Ogg Vorbis, so offline caches and some third-party rips come in this format.</li>
      <li>Linux desktops: GNOME Sound Recorder, the default export of some audio editors, and many system sounds.</li>
      <li>Messaging apps: Telegram voice notes are Opus in Ogg, usually named <code>.ogg</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Matching the bitrate</h2>
    <p>
      MP3 is an older and less efficient codec, so you cannot compare the bitrate numbers directly. MP3
      needs a little more bitrate for the same perceived quality.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Source</th><th>Approximate bitrate</th><th>MP3 setting</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Vorbis quality 3</td><td>112 kbps</td><td>128 kbps</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Vorbis quality 5 (common default)</td><td>160 kbps</td><td>192 kbps</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Vorbis quality 8</td><td>256 kbps</td><td>256–320 kbps</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Spotify high-quality stream</td><td>320 kbps Vorbis</td><td>320 kbps</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Opus voice note</td><td>24–64 kbps</td><td>128 kbps is plenty</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A higher bitrate than recommended recovers nothing, because the information the Vorbis encoder
      discarded is gone. It only makes a bigger file. A much lower bitrate adds a second round of lossy
      artefacts to the first, and the result starts to sound watery.
    </p>

    <h2>What happens to the tags</h2>
    <p>
      Ogg stores metadata as Vorbis comments: free-form <code>KEY=value</code> pairs with no fixed
      schema. MP3 uses ID3, which has a fixed set of frames. FFmpeg maps the standard fields (title,
      artist, album, date, track number, genre) onto ID3v2.3 automatically. Custom Vorbis comments from
      a game or a ripper have no ID3 equivalent and are dropped. Embedded cover art survives in most
      layouts, but not all.
    </p>

    <h2>Should you convert at all?</h2>
    <p>
      Only if a device or app cannot read Ogg. Vorbis is slightly better than MP3 at the same bitrate,
      and Opus is much better at low bitrates, so each conversion loses a little quality. If your phone,
      car and computer all play Ogg (most modern ones do), keep the originals. Convert for the one
      device that needs MP3, not the whole library.
    </p>

    <h2>Batch conversion without a meter</h2>
    <p>
      Select a whole folder of game audio or a Telegram export. The queue takes up to fifty files at a
      time, converts them one after another with a progress row each, and gives one ZIP at the end.
      Nothing is uploaded, so there is no daily limit, and the only size limit is your own memory.
    </p>

    <h2>Related tools</h2>
    <p>
      For WhatsApp, Signal and Discord audio, <a href="/opus-to-mp3">Opus to MP3</a> is the same
      converter with Opus-focused guidance. For lossless libraries,
      see <a href="/flac-to-mp3">FLAC to MP3</a>. To combine several clips first, use the
      <a href="/audio-joiner">audio joiner</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
